edit(j,5,title='Calculate Amazon contribution, inventory ROI and applicable fee taxes',summary='Include the complete per-unit cost stack and the seller\'s actual tax treatment when screening inventory.',steps=['Use the marketplace revenue calculator with the actual ASIN or dimensions, price, weight and fulfillment method.','Include landed product cost, selling and fulfillment fees, prep, labels, inbound charges, storage, expected returns and advertising where relevant.','Check fee invoices and the business\'s tax treatment; include non-recoverable tax costs and do not automatically add a blanket VAT percentage to every estimate.','Inventory ROI = contribution per unit / inventory cost per unit x 100; define the cost base consistently. £5.40 / £25 = 21.6%.','Compare contribution, inventory turnover and cash exposure together; the source\'s 20-30% thresholds are heuristics, not universal profit requirements.'],caveats=['Contribution is not final net profit after all overhead.','Use the relevant marketplace and verified tax inputs; calculator estimates may omit some costs.'])
edit(j,8,summary='Create an inbound plan from actual units, packaging and current marketplace requirements.',steps=['Start the current Send to Amazon or replenishment workflow with the correct origin and inventory.','Check category-specific prep, expiration, barcode and labeling requirements; fragility alone does not determine prep needs.','Confirm who will prepare and label each unit before dispatch; do not assume Amazon offers the services shown in an older tutorial.','Enter accurate unit counts, box contents, dimensions and weights; check current limits and exceptions for the receiving marketplace.','Choose an eligible parcel or pallet route and compare actual carrier charges.','Check charges and cancellation terms before confirming; do not assume a universal 24-hour free cancellation window.'],caveats=['Source UK dimensions, label fees and carrier recommendations are historical examples, not current global requirements.'])
